ers)", "Recent DCCC internal poll showing Knickerbocker narrowly ahead", "Heavy Democratic spending and fundraising making the race competitive"], "rationale": "Turner's incumbency and the district's Republican lean anchor the forecast, and most expert raters call the race Lean R while markets price the Democrat around one-in-three. The lone recent poll, a Democratic internal showing Knickerbocker up three, and strong late Democratic spending justify moving only modestly toward a competitive race rather than a true tossup.", "watch": "Any independent polling confirming the Democratic internal's numbers, or a major late spending imbalance, could shift this into genuine tossup territory.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Mike Turner (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Mike Turner seeks another term in a Dayton-area district that leans Republican by about four points but has become surprisingly competitive. Kristina Knickerbocker has raised over a million dollars, and Democratic groups and unions have poured outside money into the race, with headlines noting millions in total spending. A late October Democratic internal poll showed Knickerbocker ahead 47-44, though it comes from a partisan sponsor and is the only recent public survey. Most expert raters still call the race Lean R, with a few at Likely R and one at Tossup, and prediction markets give the Democrat roughly a one-in-three chance. GOP attacks on Knickerbocker's work history add friction. The race is competitive but Turner holds the edge.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1787, "margin": -5.46}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1387, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1203, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.155, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1216, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.119, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.1452, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.1169, "margin": -8.0},